+# Upgrade distribution planner draft
+
+The implemented entry point is `UpgradeDistributionPlanner.Generate(input, seed, options)` in `src/SnowPakTool.Desktop/UpgradeDistributionPlanner.cs`. It returns a complete assignment list and a report. It reads no files, mutates no saved state, stages nothing and writes no game data. It is deliberately not wired to the Upgrades tab yet.
+
+```csharp
+var input = new UpgradeDistributionInput(
+    originalPickupSlots,
+    verifiedUpgradeDefinitions,
+    effectiveObtainableTruckHomes,
+    finalizedRegionOrder);
+
+var draft = UpgradeDistributionPlanner.Generate(
+    input,
+    seed: 20261104,
+    options: new UpgradeDistributionOptions(
+        LocalMinimum: .82,
+        LocalMaximum: .91,
+        FirstRegionForeignSharedMinimum: .60,
+        FirstRegionForeignSharedMaximum: .75));
+
+// draft.Assignments: source/map/zone/array index, original ID, new ID, type, relation
+// draft.Report: targets, achieved counts, regional type totals and shortfall notes
+```
+
+## Input contract
+
+| Input | Required meaning |
+| --- | --- |
+| `Slots` | One occurrence per original pickup reward array entry. Includes source SSO, map, region, zone, reward index and original exact reward ID. The supplied slots define the shuffle scope. |
+| `Definitions` | One verified definition per reward ID, with its type and **all compatible models in the full truck catalog**. Merge repeated component IDs across definition files first. A shared part stays shared even when only one compatible model currently appears on maps. |
+| `TruckHomes` | The effective current obtainable truck placements, including randomized/draft replacements, plus direct garage/contract rewards. Mission-only and unverified placements must be excluded by the caller. Supply known homes outside the selected shuffle scope too. |
+| `RegionOrder` | The finalized progression, with every supplied pickup region represented. Its first region receives the shared-item foreign preference. |
+
+For example, Tayga 6455B has a Maine home because its contract awards a truck directly to the Garage. The caller must supply that home even though no physical obtainable scene truck exists for that reward.
+
+The confirmed Kirovets tire reward must be supplied under its exact composite ID `wheels_extend_kirovets_k7m_tire_1`, type `Tire`, compatibility `["kirovets_k7m"]`. The existing Addons catalog still exposes raw local tire names; it is not yet a complete ready-to-pass definition source. A catalog adapter must resolve composite IDs and full compatibility before the UI can invoke this planner.
+
+## Exact constraints
+
+- Every supplied pickup slot receives exactly one reward occurrence.
+- Every reward ID keeps its original occurrence count, including repeated IDs.
+- Global counts by type remain identical, including the single tire upgrade.
+- Every metal detector remains at its original source/map/zone/array index.
+- Definitions can additionally set `KeepInPlace` for any reward that should remain fixed.
+- Unresolved IDs, missing compatibility, duplicate pickup identities, duplicate definition IDs, missing region-order entries and invalid settings are rejected before a draft is returned.
+- Identical inputs/options and seed reproduce the plan. Reordering the slot, definition or home collections does not change it; changing progression order intentionally can.
+
+## Soft distribution goals
+
+The function draws one global same-region target uniformly between 82% and 91%. It uses stochastic rounding to turn that fraction into a pickup count. Only movable truck-specific rewards whose model has a known acquisition region enter that denominator. A part is local if any obtainable copy or direct award of its model exists in the pickup's region. Multiple truck copies do not multiply rewards.
+
+Truck-specific rewards with no current acquisition region form a separate unrestricted group. This includes purchase-only trucks and models absent from the current randomized acquisition snapshot. Their distribution favors spreading them in proportion to regional pickup capacity, without a home-region requirement. Adding a home automatically reclassifies the part on the next explicit generation.
+
+Shared rewards also receive a spread preference. In the first progression region only, a sampled 60–75% foreign-shared target favors shared parts with **no compatible obtainable truck in that region**. No first-region-specific foreign bonus applies to truck-specific parts. Elsewhere, shared parts receive a modest preference for local usefulness. All these terms remain soft, so a small number of slots need not achieve an exact percentage.
+
+Regional upgrade-type counts favor their original values, with deviations permitted when locality and available capacity need them. Overall type totals remain exact regardless of those regional deviations. A fixed detector occupies its original slot and is excluded from movable quotas.
+
+## Search and limits
+
+The planner tries 12 seeded starting shuffles and 12,000 pair swaps per attempt by default. A cooling acceptance rule allows temporary worse moves; it retains the best sampled assignment. Its score combines:
+
+- Global truck-specific locality count error (strongest preference).
+- Deviation from original regional type counts.
+- Regional concentration of shared and no-home truck-specific rewards.
+- First-region foreign-shared error.
+- A small random tie-break cost for variety.
+
+This is a bounded heuristic, not a proof of an optimal assignment. The report records the achieved distribution and notes any locality shortfall, including models whose home regions have no movable pickup slots. It also reports when the first-region shared preference has no candidates or receives no shared slots. It never repairs a shortfall by deleting, duplicating or inventing rewards.
+
+Nothing automatically rerolls when truck assignments change. The caller supplies a fresh snapshot on the next requested generation. Persistence, source/hash conflict checks, staging, PAK/cache-block edits and the UI action remain separate integration work.
+
+## Verification
+
+`.checks/UpgradePlannerRegression` checks exact ID/type counts, fixed detectors and explicit pins, deterministic seeds and input order, first-region shared-only bias, infeasible targets, purchase-only-to-regional transitions, multiple home regions, repeated reward IDs, unresolved inputs and empty scopes. It independently joins every reported relation to a synthetic randomized truck-home snapshot.
+
+A 166-slot example using the preserved original acquisition baseline, including the Tayga contract reward and confirmed tire, with seed `20261104` produced:
+
+| Group | Count |
+| --- | ---: |
+| Truck-specific, same region | 95 |
+| Truck-specific, elsewhere | 16 |
+| Truck-specific, no home | 16 |
+| Movable shared | 38 |
+| Fixed metal detector | 1 |
+| Total | 166 |
+
+The sampled local target was 85.14%, rounded to 95 of 111, and the plan achieved that count. Michigan was the example's first region and received five shared parts, four foreign. Its sampled foreign-shared target was 74.16%; 4/5 is an allowed soft deviation at that small sample size. This is an example fixture, not a claim to reflect the user's current randomized truck snapshot.
+
+The same reward pool with a synthetic randomized home snapshot reclassified the parts automatically: 65 truck-specific rewards had homes (56 local, nine elsewhere), 62 had no home, 38 remained movable shared and one detector stayed fixed. This demonstrates why original counts such as 96/15/16 should not be hardcoded after trucks are randomized.
+
+Run the checks with:
+
+```powershell
+dotnet run --project .checks/UpgradePlannerRegression/UpgradePlannerRegression.csproj -c Release
+```
+
+Generated example plans are under `.checks/UpgradePlannerRegression/results/` and are analysis artifacts only.
